Question : 
    Given a number n, determine whether it is a prime number or not.
    Note: A prime number is a number greater than 1 that has no positive divisors other than 1 and itself.


Code : 
    class Solution {
        static boolean isPrime(int n) {
            // code here
            int divisorCount = 0;
            for(int i=1; i*i <= n; i++) {
                if(n%i == 0) {
                    divisorCount++;
                    if(n/i != i) divisorCount++;
                }
                
                if(divisorCount > 2) break;
            }
            
            if(divisorCount != 2) return false;
            return true;
        }
    }
    

Time Complexity : O(Sqrt of n)
Space Complexity : O(1)


Explanation : 
    To find whether a number is prime or not, we have to check whether its divisor count is equals to 2 or not.
    If it equals to 2, means a prime number(1 and itself), if not, the given number is not prime.

    For that : 
    We go from 1 to sqrt of n, because factors or divisors always occur in pairs.
    In a pair of divisor, one divisor will be less than sqrt and other will be greater than sqrt.
    And when we have a divisor that is less than sqrt, we can find the other divisor greater than the sqrt.
    So there is no need to go beyond sqrt, because we get all possible divisors by going upto sqrt.

    So if i divides n perfectly, we increase divisorCount adn if i is not equal to n/i, we increase again.
    Because n/sqrt = sqrt and we shoudnt count same divisor twice, we apply the above condition.

    Once the divisorCount is greater than 2, we can break there and return false.
